WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
How do you measure one invisible molecule in a blood sample?

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:09.820
An enzyme reveals itself through its reaction, but a hormone has no reaction to

3
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:16.120
watch. To measure a silent target, scientists use an antibody-based test called an

4
00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:17.120
immunoassay.

5
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:22.160
Every immunoassay starts with an antibody, a Y-shaped protein.

6
00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:27.720
Its two arm tips bind one matching target molecule, called an antigen.

7
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.380
Other molecules in the mixture fail to fit and drift past.

8
00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.500
Once caught, the target just needs a visible readout.

9
00:00:37.483 --> 00:00:42.243
In a plate test called an ELISA, antibodies coat a well and hold the target.

10
00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:48.643
Unbound molecules wash away, and a second enzyme-linked antibody completes a

11
00:00:48.643 --> 00:00:55.223
sandwich. Its enzyme creates a colour signal, converted to concentration using a

12
00:00:55.223 --> 00:00:56.983
standard curve of known amounts.

13
00:00:58.017 --> 00:01:02.517
When targets sit on cells or bacteria, antibodies need no enzyme.

14
00:01:03.917 --> 00:01:08.537
Each antibody's two arms link neighbouring cells into visible clumps, called

15
00:01:08.537 --> 00:01:14.437
agglutination. Diluting serum in steps finds the titer, the highest dilution that

16
00:01:14.437 --> 00:01:20.717
still clumps. More antibody survives more dilution, so the titer is a relative

17
00:01:20.717 --> 00:01:21.817
measure of antibody.

18
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.080
Yet one signal can still mislead.

19
00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:31.660
Suppose an ELISA screen of serum for antibodies against HIV turns positive.

20
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.820
ELISA is sensitive but less specific: its colour cannot show which protein was

21
00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:42.980
bound. How can the lab confirm the antibodies match true viral proteins?

22
00:01:48.633 --> 00:01:53.173
Classic HIV testing confirmed positives with a western blot.

23
00:01:54.533 --> 00:01:58.813
A gel separates viral proteins by size, then they move onto a membrane.

24
00:02:00.213 --> 00:02:02.273
The patient's antibodies are laid on it.

25
00:02:03.373 --> 00:02:08.673
A true positive lights bands at the expected sizes of specific viral proteins.

26
00:02:09.817 --> 00:02:13.677
Even a confirmed test only measures the molecule it binds.

27
00:02:15.057 --> 00:02:20.117
An ELISA tracks antibody in blood plasma, not the memory B cells held in reserve.

28
00:02:21.577 --> 00:02:25.677
Memory B cells can persist or even rise while antibody falls.

29
00:02:26.977 --> 00:02:31.817
Neither readout measures protection, which needs its own endpoint, like reinfection

30
00:02:31.817 --> 00:02:32.817
rates.

31
00:02:33.117 --> 00:02:34.877
Clumping tests made history.

32
00:02:36.197 --> 00:02:39.837
In 1901, Karl Landsteiner mixed serum and red cells.

33
00:02:41.237 --> 00:02:48.637
Clumping sorted people into groups A, B, and C, now called O. In 1956, Glick, Chang,

34
00:02:48.757 --> 00:02:52.097
and Jaap studied the bursa, a lymphoid pouch in chickens.

35
00:02:53.437 --> 00:02:55.597
They immunized birds with and without it.

36
00:02:55.597 --> 00:03:02.017
Clumping titers showed most birds without it made little or no detectable antibody.

37
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:08.300
In 2012, Walker and colleagues asked what drives the hourly pulses of

38
00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.480
corticosterone, a rat hormone.

39
00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:16.620
A natural guess: each pulse follows a burst of CRH from the hypothalamus.

40
00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:23.500
They dripped in CRH at a low, steady rate, measuring corticosterone every five

41
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:24.880
minutes by immunoassay.

42
00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.960
It still pulsed about once an hour.

43
00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.100
So a pituitary-adrenal loop can make its own rhythm.
